{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013QcNFiiK6Kqrpc1WedSDSn","type":"tool_result","content":"1625\tdef refuse(header: str, problems: list[str], guidance: str) -> int:\n1626\t    \"\"\"The one refusal path: exit 1, one message per problem.\n1627\t\n1628\t    Every refusal that has problems to enumerate goes through here — the two\n1629\t    story-artifact refusals, the clean-tree one, and l5-plan's plan-time\n1630\t    validation — so the shape stays a single code path rather than a copy per\n1631\t    reason. What differs between them is the sentence above the list and the\n1632\t    sentence below it, which is what a caller supplies.\n1633\t\n1634\t    It is public because plan time and pre-flight must print a given problem\n1635\t    identically: the same defect in the same artifact produces the same text\n1636\t    whether it is caught when the artifact is written or when it is run.\n1637\t    \"\"\"\n1638\t    print(header, file=sys.stderr)\n1639\t    for problem in problems:\n1640\t        print(f\"  - {problem}\", file=sys.stderr)\n1641\t    print(guidance, file=sys.stderr)\n1642\t    return 1\n1643\t\n1644\t\n1645\tdef refuse_bad_story(story_path: Path, problems: list[str]) -> int:\n1646\t    return refuse(\n1647\t        f\"{story_path} is not a valid story artifact:\",\n1648\t        problems,\n1649\t        \"Fix the artifact or re-run planning before executing the story.\",\n1650\t    )\n1651\t\n1652\t\n1653\tdef _refuse_dirty_tree(target_root: Path, paths: list[str]) -> int:\n1654\t    \"\"\"Refuse a run whose target tree already holds work no stage produced.\n1655\t\n1656\t    The friction here is the point of contact a developer meets most often, so\n1657\t    the message names every dirty path and says what clears it.\n1658\t    \"\"\"\n1659\t    return refuse(\n1660\t        f\"{target_root} has uncommitted changes, so a run starting here could \"\n1661\t        f\"not establish that what it commits is what it produced:\",\n1662\t        paths,\n1663\t        \"Commit or stash them, then run the story again.\",\n1664\t    )\n1665\t\n1666\t\n1667\tdef _refuse_finished_branch(branch: str, run_dir: Path, commits: list[str]) -> int:\n1668\t    \"\"\"Refuse a run whose branch already carries the story's finished work.\n1669\t\n1670\t    The refusal names what it found and what to do about each of the two things\n1671\t    a re-run would otherwise silently sit on top of: the branch, whose reset or\n1672\t    deletion discards work a run finished, and the run directory, which is\n1673\t    gitignored and therefore lost rather than merely uncommitted.\n1674\t    \"\"\"\n1675\t    return refuse(\n1676\t        f\"Branch {branch} already carries a completion commit for this story, \"\n1677\t        f\"so a run starting here would re-run a story that has already \"\n1678\t        f\"finished:\",\n1679\t        commits,\n1680\t        f\"A completion commit reachable from the branch you plan to run from \"\n1681\t        f\"means the story has already shipped; the right response is a new \"\n1682\t        f\"story describing what is still wanted, not a reset. To genuinely run \"\n1683\t        f\"this one again, reset or delete {branch} — which discards the \"\n1684\t        f\"finished work — and delete {run_dir}, which is gitignored and will \"\n1685\t        f\"not come back. Archive either to .harness/runs-archive/ first if it \"\n1686\t        f\"is worth keeping.\",\n1687\t    )\n1688\t\n1689\t\n1690\tdef _commits_the_tree_it_ends_on(escalate):\n1691\t    \"\"\"Commit the escalated work after the escalation has finished writing.\n1692\t\n1693\t    The work commit has to be the last thing that happens: every file the\n1694\t    escalation writes — state.json, both renderings of the event stream, the\n1695\t    escalation summary — belongs inside it, or a repository that tracks its run\n1696\t    directory is left dirty by the very writes that record the escalation, and\n1697\t    the checkout this story exists to make safe is refused. Wrapping is how the\n1698\t    ordering is expressed without moving the escalation's own writing around,\n1699\t    whose last act is the summary a separate request owns.\n1700\t    \"\"\"\n1701\t\n1702\t    @functools.wraps(escalate)\n1703\t    def escalate_and_commit(\n1704\t        run_dir: Path,\n1705\t        state: RunState,\n1706\t        reason: str,\n1707\t        *,\n1708\t        target_root: Path,\n1709\t        harness_root: Path,\n1710\t        **event_fields,\n1711\t    ) -> int:\n1712\t        code = escalate(\n1713\t            run_dir,\n1714\t            state,\n1715\t            reason,\n1716\t            target_root=target_root,\n1717\t            harness_root=harness_root,\n1718\t            **event_fields,\n1719\t        )\n1720\t        if state.escalation_commit:\n1721\t            commit_escalated_tree(target_root, state, reason)\n1722\t        return code\n1723\t\n1724\t    return escalate_and_commit\n1725\t\n1726\t\n1727\t@_commits_the_tree_it_ends_on\n1728\tdef _escalate(\n1729\t    run_dir: Path,\n1730\t    state: RunState,\n1731\t    reason: str,\n1732\t    *,\n1733\t    target_root: Path,\n1734\t    harness_root: Path,\n1735\t    **event_fields,\n1736\t) -> int:\n1737\t    \"\"\"End the run, recording the escalation in both renderings.\n1738\t\n1739\t    A run that failed must be as reconstructable as one that passed, so the\n1740\t    history ends with this entry. Callers forward whatever structured fields\n1741\t    the escalation has — the stage's elapsed time, the verifier's outcome,\n1742\t    the decision that routed here — through event_fields.\n1743\t\n1744\t    The run's record of the escalation is committed here, and the sha of that\n1745\t    commit is written into state.json for the commit that follows to carry —\n1746\t    the wrapper above commits the tree once this has written everything, so\n1747\t    that the escalation's own evidence is inside the commit and the tree it\n1748\t    leaves is clean. The harness revision is recorded here too, for the same\n1749\t    reader: a resume can then tell whether the harness itself has changed\n1750\t    since.\n1751\t    \"\"\"\n1752\t    state.harness_revision = _revision(harness_root)\n1753\t    state.status = \"escalated\"\n1754\t    save_state(run_dir, state)\n1755\t    append_event(\n1756\t        run_dir,\n1757\t        f\"escalated: {reason}\",\n1758\t        kind=\"escalated\",\n1759\t        stage=state.current_stage or None,\n1760\t        **event_fields,\n1761\t    )\n1762\t    state.escalation_commit = commit_escalated_work(\n1763\t        target_root, state, reason, run_dir=run_dir\n1764\t    )\n1765\t    if state.escalation_commit:\n1766\t        save_state(run_dir, state)\n1767\t    summary = escalation_summary(run_dir, state, reason)\n1768\t    (run_dir / \"escalation-summary.md\").write_text(summary, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n1769\t    return 2\n1770\t\n1771\t\n1772\tdef _complete(run_dir: Path, state: RunState, story: dict, target_root: Path) -> int:\n1773\t    state.status = \"completed\"\n1774\t    state.current_stage = \"\"\n1775\t    save_state(run_dir, state)\n1776\t    # The schema marks title required and the run cannot reach here without\n1777\t    # having passed validation, so a missing title is a loud failure rather\n1778\t    # than a silently empty report.\n1779\t    title = story[\"story\"][\"title\"]\n1780\t    report = (\n1781\t        f\"# {state.story_id} Completion Report\\n\\n\"\n1782\t        f\"## Story\\n{title}\\n\\n\"\n1783\t        f\"## Outcome\\nCompleted on branch {state.branch} after \"\n1784\t        f\"{state.retry_count} retr{'y' if state.retry_count == 1 else 'ies'}.\\n\\n\"\n1785\t        f\"## Evidence\\n\"\n1786\t        f\"- test-results.json\\n\"\n1787\t        f\"- verification/iteration-{state.verification_iterations}.json (passed)\\n\"\n1788\t        f\"- documentation-report.md\\n\\n\"\n1789\t        f\"## Next Step\\nReview the branch and merge it when you accept the story.\\n\"\n1790\t    )\n1791\t    (run_dir / \"completion-report.md\").write_text(report, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n1792\t    _git(target_root, \"add\", \"-A\")\n1793\t    _git(target_root, \"commit\", \"-m\", completion_commit_message(state, title))\n1794\t    append_event(\n1795\t        run_dir,\n1796\t        f\"story completed on branch {state.branch}\",\n1797\t        kind=\"story-completed\",\n1798\t    )\n1799\t    return 0\n1800\t\n1801\t\n1802\tdef run_story(\n1803\t    story_id: str,\n1804\t    harness_root: Path,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84f4a87c-b999-457a-947f-f1281f3fb1f8","uuid":"f0808430-b341-4a64-99b0-039850037d2c","timestamp":"2026-08-11T02:07:37.484Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jerodw/Work/AgenticProgramming/level-five/orchestration/story_coordinator.py","content":"def refuse(header: str, problems: list[str], guidance: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The one refusal path: exit 1, one message per problem.\n\n    Every refusal that has problems to enumerate goes through here — the two\n    story-artifact refusals, the clean-tree one, and l5-plan's plan-time\n    validation — so the shape stays a single code path rather than a copy per\n    reason. What differs between them is the sentence above the list and the\n    sentence below it, which is what a caller supplies.\n\n    It is public because plan time and pre-flight must print a given problem\n    identically: the same defect in the same artifact produces the same text\n    whether it is caught when the artifact is written or when it is run.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(header, file=sys.stderr)\n    for problem in problems:\n        print(f\"  - {problem}\", file=sys.stderr)\n    print(guidance, file=sys.stderr)\n    return 1\n\n\ndef refuse_bad_story(story_path: Path, problems: list[str]) -> int:\n    return refuse(\n        f\"{story_path} is not a valid story artifact:\",\n        problems,\n        \"Fix the artifact or re-run planning before executing the story.\",\n    )\n\n\ndef _refuse_dirty_tree(target_root: Path, paths: list[str]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Refuse a run whose target tree already holds work no stage produced.\n\n    The friction here is the point of contact a developer meets most often, so\n    the message names every dirty path and says what clears it.\n    \"\"\"\n    return refuse(\n        f\"{target_root} has uncommitted changes, so a run starting here could \"\n        f\"not establish that what it commits is what it produced:\",\n        paths,\n        \"Commit or stash them, then run the story again.\",\n    )\n\n\ndef _refuse_finished_branch(branch: str, run_dir: Path, commits: list[str]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Refuse a run whose branch already carries the story's finished work.\n\n    The refusal names what it found and what to do about each of the two things\n    a re-run would otherwise silently sit on top of: the branch, whose reset or\n    deletion discards work a run finished, and the run directory, which is\n    gitignored and therefore lost rather than merely uncommitted.\n    \"\"\"\n    return refuse(\n        f\"Branch {branch} already carries a completion commit for this story, \"\n        f\"so a run starting here would re-run a story that has already \"\n        f\"finished:\",\n        commits,\n        f\"A completion commit reachable from the branch you plan to run from \"\n        f\"means the story has already shipped; the right response is a new \"\n        f\"story describing what is still wanted, not a reset. To genuinely run \"\n        f\"this one again, reset or delete {branch} — which discards the \"\n        f\"finished work — and delete {run_dir}, which is gitignored and will \"\n        f\"not come back. Archive either to .harness/runs-archive/ first if it \"\n        f\"is worth keeping.\",\n    )\n\n\ndef _commits_the_tree_it_ends_on(escalate):\n    \"\"\"Commit the escalated work after the escalation has finished writing.\n\n    The work commit has to be the last thing that happens: every file the\n    escalation writes — state.json, both renderings of the event stream, the\n    escalation summary — belongs inside it, or a repository that tracks its run\n    directory is left dirty by the very writes that record the escalation, and\n    the checkout this story exists to make safe is refused. Wrapping is how the\n    ordering is expressed without moving the escalation's own writing around,\n    whose last act is the summary a separate request owns.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @functools.wraps(escalate)\n    def escalate_and_commit(\n        run_dir: Path,\n        state: RunState,\n        reason: str,\n        *,\n        target_root: Path,\n        harness_root: Path,\n        **event_fields,\n    ) -> int:\n        code = escalate(\n            run_dir,\n            state,\n            reason,\n            target_root=target_root,\n            harness_root=harness_root,\n            **event_fields,\n        )\n        if state.escalation_commit:\n            commit_escalated_tree(target_root, state, reason)\n        return code\n\n    return escalate_and_commit\n\n\n@_commits_the_tree_it_ends_on\ndef _escalate(\n    run_dir: Path,\n    state: RunState,\n    reason: str,\n    *,\n    target_root: Path,\n    harness_root: Path,\n    **event_fields,\n) -> int:\n    \"\"\"End the run, recording the escalation in both renderings.\n\n    A run that failed must be as reconstructable as one that passed, so the\n    history ends with this entry. Callers forward whatever structured fields\n    the escalation has — the stage's elapsed time, the verifier's outcome,\n    the decision that routed here — through event_fields.\n\n    The run's record of the escalation is committed here, and the sha of that\n    commit is written into state.json for the commit that follows to carry —\n    the wrapper above commits the tree once this has written everything, so\n    that the escalation's own evidence is inside the commit and the tree it\n    leaves is clean. The harness revision is recorded here too, for the same\n    reader: a resume can then tell whether the harness itself has changed\n    since.\n    \"\"\"\n    state.harness_revision = _revision(harness_root)\n    state.status = \"escalated\"\n    save_state(run_dir, state)\n    append_event(\n        run_dir,\n        f\"escalated: {reason}\",\n        kind=\"escalated\",\n        stage=state.current_stage or None,\n        **event_fields,\n    )\n    state.escalation_commit = commit_escalated_work(\n        target_root, state, reason, run_dir=run_dir\n    )\n    if state.escalation_commit:\n        save_state(run_dir, state)\n    summary = escalation_summary(run_dir, state, reason)\n    (run_dir / \"escalation-summary.md\").write_text(summary, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    return 2\n\n\ndef _complete(run_dir: Path, state: RunState, story: dict, target_root: Path) -> int:\n    state.status = \"completed\"\n    state.current_stage = \"\"\n    save_state(run_dir, state)\n    # The schema marks title required and the run cannot reach here without\n    # having passed validation, so a missing title is a loud failure rather\n    # than a silently empty report.\n    title = story[\"story\"][\"title\"]\n    report = (\n        f\"# {state.story_id} Completion Report\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## Story\\n{title}\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## Outcome\\nCompleted on branch {state.branch} after \"\n        f\"{state.retry_count} retr{'y' if state.retry_count == 1 else 'ies'}.\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## Evidence\\n\"\n        f\"- test-results.json\\n\"\n        f\"- verification/iteration-{state.verification_iterations}.json (passed)\\n\"\n        f\"- documentation-report.md\\n\\n\"\n        f\"## Next Step\\nReview the branch and merge it when you accept the story.\\n\"\n    )\n    (run_dir / \"completion-report.md\").write_text(report, encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    _git(target_root, \"add\", \"-A\")\n    _git(target_root, \"commit\", \"-m\", completion_commit_message(state, title))\n    append_event(\n        run_dir,\n        f\"story completed on branch {state.branch}\",\n        kind=\"story-completed\",\n    )\n    return 0\n\n\ndef run_story(\n    story_id: str,\n    harness_root: Path,","numLines":180,"startLine":1625,"totalLines":2367}}}
